# Bash Fundamentals

## What exactly is Bash?

Bash = Bourne Again Shell.

When you open your Ubuntu terminal and type:

"ls" or, "cd /var/www"

you're interacting with a shell.

Bash is one of the most commonly used shells on Linux.

## You can check yours:

> echo $SHELL

## Also check the Bash version:

> bash --version

# Terminal vs Shell vs Bash

Terminal:
The application/window where you type commands.

Shell:
The program that receives your commands and executes them.

Bash:
One particular shell.

# What is #!/bin/bash?

This line: #!/bin/bash
is called a shebang.

It tells Linux: "Use /bin/bash to execute this file." It's extremely common at the beginning of Bash scripts.

# How to make the script executable

> ./<file-name>.sh

# Important difference

You now know two ways to run a Bash script.

Method 1: bash hello.sh
You're explicitly telling Bash: Execute this file using Bash.

Method 2: ./hello.sh
You're telling Linux: Execute this file.
Linux then looks at: #!/bin/bash
and knows to use Bash.
